  ('5eb0a8e2-45c3-576a-8139-5e96189cafaa', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null, 'medium',
   $q$What does Orrin's claim that "The river is in the wrong place" reveal about the conflict that develops the theme?$q$, $q$[{"text":"He is worried that Liesl has secretly changed his maps.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"He is joking with the travelers to calm their frustration.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"He trusts his finished map more than the changed world, so pride blocks him from seeing the truth.","is_correct":true},{"text":"He has evidence that floods in the valley always reverse within a season.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$He trusts his finished map more than the changed world, so pride blocks him from seeing the truth.$q$, $q$Blaming the river instead of the map shows Orrin placing his work above reality, the flaw the fable corrects. He offers no evidence that the river will return; he only insists his map "was drawn with great care."$q$, 'draft', 'bab7329c-41a8-5098-83e6-e160f484dcd1')
