insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9aea529c-f612-53d7-9bc0-132ec2e4b363', 'rg11-theme-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Tables Under the Sycamore$q$, $q$Elan Brightwater had a chess rating that put him among the top five players his age in the state, and he carried it the way some people carry an expensive jacket. When his family moved to Corvallis Street in June, he found the stone tables by the fountain on his second day, where a few older men played fast games under a sycamore tree.

He sat down across from a retired bus mechanic named Mr. Halvorsen, who played with his sleeves rolled up and talked the whole time. Elan won in nineteen moves. He won the next game in twenty-two. He did not say anything unkind, but he did not need to. He reset the pieces with the bored speed of someone who had already decided what the afternoon was worth.

Mr. Halvorsen only chuckled. "You play like a book," he said. "Good book. Do you know why you moved your bishop there?"

"It's the main line," Elan said.

"That's what you read. I asked what you know."

Elan came back the next day, mostly to prove a point. Mr. Halvorsen did not play him. Instead he set up strange positions from games he had watched in the park over forty years and asked Elan to find a plan, not a move. Elan found the answers slowly, and some of them he did not find at all. Twice he walked home annoyed. Both times he came back.

By August the men under the sycamore called him "the kid with the questions." His rating barely changed that summer. But at the state tournament in the fall, facing an opponent who had prepared a surprise opening, Elan sat in a position no book had shown him and felt, for the first time, not panic but curiosity. He thought of the sycamore and the rolled sleeves, and he asked himself what the position wanted.

He drew the game. It was the best draw of his life, and he told Mr. Halvorsen so before he told anyone else.$q$, 'draft')
