  ('1be89ff4-1b3f-5e4c-9624-2247c295b36d', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The words "philanthropist" and "misanthrope" both contain anthropo. Which statement best explains how the passage uses the difference between them?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The first parts reverse the attitude toward people, and Clara learns her neighbor acted more like the first word than the second.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The first parts change the time period, and Clara learns her neighbor lived a century earlier than she thought.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-root-meaning"},{"text":"The first parts change the subject from people to animals, and Clara learns her neighbor cared for animals.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"The first parts mean the same thing, and Clara learns that both Mr. Castellanos and the neighbor were donors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-affix-change"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The first parts reverse the attitude toward people, and Clara learns her neighbor acted more like the first word than the second.$q$, $q$Phil (love) and mis (hatred) give opposite attitudes toward humans, and Clara discovers that the supposed misanthrope "quietly repaired the school's broken desks," a generous act. Nothing in the passage says the neighbor gave money, so calling both men donors misreads the text and ignores the opposite prefixes.$q$, 'draft', '2b8d33eb-bae9-53db-a112-7bad5ec265b4')
