values
  ('439aa963-eada-5fbd-baf3-62d81fa55142', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The passage uses "magnitude" and "magnify," which share the root magn, "great." Which statement best explains how their suffixes change meaning and part of speech?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both are adjectives, and each describes how dangerous a particular neighborhood is.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-part-of-speech"},{"text":"Magnitude is a verb meaning to grow larger; magnify is a noun naming the size of a quake.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-parts-of-speech"},{"text":"Both are nouns, and each names the amount of energy an earthquake releases at its source.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-affix-change"},{"text":"Magnitude is a noun naming how great something is; magnify is a verb meaning to make something greater.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Magnitude is a noun naming how great something is; magnify is a verb meaning to make something greater.$q$, $q$The suffix -tude forms a noun (a quality), while -ify forms a verb (to make), so soil can "magnify the motion" while each quake has one magnitude. Calling both nouns ignores how -ify turns the root into an action.$q$, 'draft', 'b645b244-bb93-53d6-81a6-6f139575506a')
