-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3ad6a6b9-6300-5439-8fed-2bc15355f38a', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Take Down the Sign$q$, $q$For forty years, the Harwick Museum of Art has posted a small sign beside every gallery door that reads No Photography. The rule made sense when cameras relied on bright flashbulbs, which can fade delicate pigments over time. It makes far less sense today, and the museum's board should allow visitors to take photographs without flash in every permanent gallery.

The original concern was light damage, but that concern no longer applies to ordinary phone cameras. In a study at the Tavira Conservation Institute, researchers measured the light given off when visitors photographed a test canvas without flash. The total exposure from ten thousand such photographs was smaller than the exposure from a single minute under the gallery's own ceiling lamps. A rule meant to protect paintings now protects them from almost nothing.

Allowing photography would also draw visitors. When the Calder Bay Museum lifted its own ban three years ago, attendance rose by 18 percent the following year. That was also the year Calder Bay opened its new sculpture wing, but the timing of the policy change is hard to ignore.

Some staff members worry that visitors will stare at their screens instead of the art. I understand the concern, yet in my own experience, photographing a painting makes me look at it more closely, not less. I notice the brushwork as I frame the shot, and I return to the image at home. Surely other visitors feel the same way.

Others raise the question of copyright. Nearly every work in the permanent collection was made before 1900, and the gift shop already sells postcards and posters of the most popular pieces. If the museum is comfortable printing these images and selling them, it can hardly object when a visitor makes a private copy on a phone.

Museums once guarded their collections by keeping people at a distance. Today they thrive by inviting people in. Taking down a forty-year-old sign is a small step that would make Harwick a more welcoming place.$q$, 'draft')
