values
  ('60ed3273-ab31-5eae-aff0-5849b26412b1', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The argument that individual errors "tend to cancel out" relies on which premise?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Most volunteers have years of training in identifying birds.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unnecessary-assumption"},{"text":"Volunteers' mistakes are random rather than leaning one way.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Rare birds are recorded more often in large groups of observers.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Professional biologists make more errors than volunteers do.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Volunteers' mistakes are random rather than leaning one way.$q$, $q$Errors cancel only if high counts are balanced by low ones, which requires that they do not all lean in the same direction. The rare species finding actually shows a one-way error, since volunteers consistently miss those birds, so the premise holds best for common species.$q$, 'draft', '0418763f-6853-5233-9321-066a2c197ede')
