   $q$Why does the author open the essay with a description of Teodor Halvorsen's morning routine?$q$, $q$[{"text":"To entertain readers with a humorous story before she turns to a serious scientific topic","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"To prove with statistics that Teodor's records are more accurate than any satellite measurement","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-evidence-type"},{"text":"To suggest that only retired people have enough free time to take part in weather research","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralizes"},{"text":"To ground her argument in one specific, believable person before she makes a broader claim about volunteers","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$To ground her argument in one specific, believable person before she makes a broader claim about volunteers$q$, $q$The concrete routine gives readers a real face for the abstract idea of volunteer data, and the essay returns to him when it widens to "thousands of volunteers like Teodor." The opening offers no statistics, so it cannot be statistical proof.$q$, 'draft', '55fa7369-65d4-5970-b16d-012a6bc69da2')
