-- Passage rg12-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f866cb3d-2962-5033-be6d-0a5486cd3d02', 'rg12-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Forty Steps$q$, $q$From an essay by Marguerite Osei in a city magazine's column on everyday life.

There is a small test of character that most of us take several times a week, usually without noticing. You have finished loading groceries into your car. The cart is empty. The return corral is forty steps away, across a windy lot, and no one is watching. Nothing bad will happen to you if you leave the cart beside your bumper. Nobody will fine you, and nobody will know your name.

I have been thinking about that cart for years, and I have come to believe it tells us more than it seems to. Most of the rules we follow are enforced by something: a fine, a teacher, a referee, the disapproval of a friend. Returning the cart is enforced by nothing at all. It is one of the rare everyday choices where the only reason to do the right thing is that it is the right thing.

I want to be fair here. There are good reasons a person might leave a cart: a sleeping infant in the back seat, a bad knee, a sudden storm. I am not writing about those people, and I suspect they feel worse about it than anyone watching. I am writing about the rest of us, on an ordinary afternoon, with two working legs and nowhere urgent to be.

What happens to that abandoned cart? It drifts into a parking space, dents a door, or waits for a teenager in a reflective vest to chase it across the lot. The cost is small. That is precisely why it is revealing. Large decisions are made with an audience in mind. Small ones show what we do when the audience has gone home.

I do not think anyone is a bad person for leaving a cart once. But habits are built from moments like this one, forty steps at a time. Walk the cart back. Nobody will thank you, and that is the whole point.$q$, 'draft')
