-- Passage rg12-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4589bd8d-5c00-5678-a18c-486ae90cdcf4', 'rg12-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Letter D$q$, $q$By the time the conductor tapped his stand, everyone in the Riverside Youth Orchestra had an opinion about Teodora Vasquez, and none of them had met her.

"She left a conservatory after one semester," said the oboist, adjusting her reed. "Just walked out."

"I heard she won't play anything written after 1900," said a violist.

"I heard she tunes the whole section herself, one by one, like a drill sergeant," said Felix Amankwah, who sat at the last stand of cellos and had sat there for two seasons. He said it lightly, but his stomach tightened. The new principal cellist would decide, among other things, whether he ever moved up.

The chair at the front of the section stayed empty through the warm-up and through the first run of the overture. The conductor did not explain. When the door finally opened, the person who came in was shorter than Felix had pictured, carrying a cello case covered in faded stickers from a children's music camp. She whispered an apology to the conductor, sat down, and played the rest of the overture without once looking up.

At the break she did not tune anyone. She walked to the back of the section and asked Felix what he thought of the slow passage at letter D.

He told her, surprised to find he had an answer: the section rushed it every time because nobody could hear the harp.

"Then play it with me from up front," she said. "I need someone who listens."

When rehearsal ended, Felix stayed to help stack chairs. Teodora was still in her seat, her hands pressed flat on her knees.

"I was late because I sat in the parking lot for twenty minutes," she said quietly. "I almost did not come in."$q$, 'draft')
