   $q$How does Teodora's final admission about the parking lot change the meaning of her earlier behavior?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Her lateness now reads as proof that the conservatory rumor was true and she often quits.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Her request at the break now reads as a test meant to embarrass Felix in front of the section.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Her lateness and refusal to look up now read as nerves, not arrogance, linking her to Felix's own anxiety.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Her quiet manner now reads as anger at the conductor for starting the overture without her.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Her lateness and refusal to look up now read as nerves, not arrogance, linking her to Felix's own anxiety.$q$, $q$Sitting in the parking lot and almost not coming in reveals fear, which recasts the late entrance and downcast playing and echoes Felix's tightened stomach. Nothing suggests she meant to embarrass him; she says she needs "someone who listens."$q$, 'draft', '4589bd8d-5c00-5678-a18c-486ae90cdcf4')
