insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f3773370-9e38-55ca-b9b8-ff228a411527', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The River Returns (1990s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Count (a story, 1997)

The year after the old mill dam came down, the watershed council asked for volunteers to count herring at the Corrow River fish ladder. Yusuf Delacroix signed up mostly because it counted as community service, and because the shift started after school.

His partner was Mrs. Odette Fairweather, who had worked at the mill for thirty years. She brought a thermos and a clicker counter and sat on an overturned bucket as though she had done this all her life.

"When I was your age," she said, "this river ran a different color every week, depending on what the dye vats were doing. Blue on Mondays. Red by Thursday. We used to bet on it."

For nine afternoons they counted nothing. Yusuf did homework on his knee. Mrs. Fairweather watched the water.

On the tenth afternoon, a silver flicker turned in the shallow pool below the ladder, then another, then a dozen. Mrs. Fairweather pressed the clicker so fast that she laughed out loud and handed it to him.

"You do it," she said. "My hands are shaking."

Yusuf clicked until the light went orange. He wrote the total on the council's form, 214, and underneath it, without quite knowing why, he added: first year.

Text 2: What the River Remembers (a poem, 1997)

For ninety years I carried what I was given:
The dye, the ash, the wheel's unending churn.
I learned to run in colors not my own
And thought that I had nothing left to learn.

Then they took down the wall across my throat.
I ran too fast that spring, and then ran clear.
The stones remembered how to shape my voice.
The herons came to stand and wait for what was near.

And somewhere in the salt, a memory
Older than mills turned slowly toward the shore,
And swam against me, eager, into me,
As if it had been called by name once more.$q$, 'draft')
