insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9c61e3ac-7ae5-5e23-8b44-5526e308c0fc', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Between Two Languages (2000s)$q$, $q$Text 1: The Notebook of Lost Words (a story, 2006)

Two years after her family moved, Lien Tran discovered that she could no longer write a full letter to her grandmother without stopping. Words she had used every day as a child now hovered just out of reach: the name for the clay pot on the back step, the phrase for the sound rain made on a tin roof.

At school, she was proud of how quickly her English had grown. Her teachers praised her essays. But each time she sat down to write home, she felt like someone returning to a house where the furniture had been quietly moved.

So she began a notebook. Whenever a word escaped her, she wrote its English meaning on the left side of the page and left the right side blank. On the weekly phone call, she would read the list aloud, and her grandmother, laughing, would fill each gap.

By spring, the notebook held more than two hundred words. Lien no longer thought of it as a record of what she had lost. It had become a record of what she had gotten back.

Text 2: Lessons on Saturday (a story, 2006)

Gabriel Mendoza-Price had grown up hearing his grandfather's language at every holiday, but he understood only scraps of it. At sixteen, he enrolled in a Saturday class at the community center, where the younger students corrected his pronunciation with obvious delight.

The first time he tried a full sentence with his grandfather, he meant to say that the coffee smelled wonderful. Instead, judging by his grandfather's face, he said something about the coffee being a wonderful horse.

His grandfather laughed until he had to sit down. Then he took Gabriel's hand, repeated the sentence correctly, slowly, twice, and waited.

Gabriel tried again. This time it came out right, and his grandfather answered with a long story about a horse he had owned as a boy, told in words Gabriel only half understood. He did not interrupt. For the first time, he did not need to understand every word to know what was being given to him.$q$, 'draft')
