-- Passage rg12-comparing-texts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('02105ba7-b9f2-5a4c-a9c7-a6649a325864', 'rg12-comparing-texts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Looking for Stars (2020s)$q$, $q$Text 1: Ninety Miles from the Glow (a story, 2023)

Nadia Ferreira had promised her younger brother, Theo, a real night sky for his tenth birthday, so in late August she drove them ninety miles to a dark-sky park where the rangers kept every light shielded and red. They arrived at dusk, ate sandwiches on the hood of the car, and waited.

At first Theo was unimpressed. Then, as their eyes adjusted, the sky began to fill. Stars appeared behind stars. A pale band of light stretched from one horizon to the other, so thick in places that it looked like a cloud.

"Is that smoke?" Theo whispered.

"That's our galaxy," Nadia said. "We're inside it, looking along the edge."

They lay on a blanket until past midnight. On the drive home, Theo fell asleep, and Nadia watched the sky lighten mile by mile, not toward dawn but toward the orange dome of the city. By the time they reached their street, she could count the stars on two hands. She wondered how many children on their block had never seen what Theo had just seen, and whether they even knew it was missing.

Text 2: Inventory of a City Sky (a poem, 2023)

From my fire escape at ten o'clock, I count:
one planet, steady as a porch light,
three jets blinking their slow way west,
a satellite sliding like a bead on a wire,
and the moon, rubbed thin, above the parking garage.

I know the rest is there. I've read the charts.
The glow of a million kitchens hides it,
and I am one of the kitchens.

Still, I keep my inventory.
One planet is not nothing.
One thin moon is not nothing.
I have learned to love a sky
the way you love a friend who calls rarely:
holding on to every word.$q$, 'draft')
