   $q$How does the final paragraph connect the 1884 rule back to the opening of the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The rule proved that coal tar was dangerous, which explains why Halvik's first experiment failed as medicine.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"The rule ended the dye industry, so Marrowford returned to the medical research Halvik had first pursued.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The rule required factories to hire physicians, who then finished the medicine Halvik had started.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The rule pushed chemists to study their waste, which yielded medicines and so fulfilled Halvik's original goal indirectly.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The rule pushed chemists to study their waste, which yielded medicines and so fulfilled Halvik's original goal indirectly.$q$, $q$The last paragraph shows the rule forcing chemists to analyze waste, which produced an antiseptic, and it closes by noting that Halvik's "failed medicine" became a source of "real medicines after all." The dye works were not closed; they earned as much from byproducts as from dye.$q$, 'draft', 'a2cbc400-e47b-5386-892a-8feb50abfc3f')
