-- Passage rg12-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b1e57b45-2b05-5e04-9c14-814b4e94f87e', 'rg12-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Sharper Glass in Ostervik$q$, $q$In the 1820s, the microscopes used by physicians in the fictional port city of Ostervik showed a blurry fringe of color around every object. The problem lay in the glass: a simple lens bends red and blue light by different amounts, so the colors of an image never come into focus at the same point. Physicians who looked at blood or tissue could see shapes, but not the fine details inside them.

A lens grinder named Matthias Oyelaran spent eight years on the problem. He found that pairing two lenses made from different kinds of glass could cancel most of the color fringe, because the second lens bent the colors back in the opposite direction. The idea was not entirely new, but no one had been able to grind such lenses small enough for a microscope. Oyelaran's contribution was a set of polishing tools and a method for checking each lens against a printed grid of fine lines.

His sharper microscopes reached the Ostervik medical school in 1834, where a young instructor named Celestine Abara used one to study samples from patients with a fever that spread along the waterfront each summer. With the old instruments, the samples looked like ordinary fluid. Through Oyelaran's lenses, Abara saw tiny rod-shaped bodies that appeared in sick patients and not in healthy ones.

Abara could not prove that the rods caused the fever, and many of her colleagues doubted that anything so small could harm a person. Her drawings, however, were precise enough that other researchers could look for the same shapes. Over the next twenty years, instructors in three other cities reported matching observations, and the growing body of evidence persuaded the city's health board to clean and cover the waterfront drains where the rods were found in large numbers.

Summer fevers in Ostervik fell by more than half within a decade of the drainage work. The health board's report credited Abara's observations, but Abara herself, in a lecture late in her life, pointed out that her discovery depended on a craftsman who had never treated a patient and on dozens of researchers whose names the report did not mention.$q$, 'draft')
