-- Passage rg12-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('808ede3a-4c5b-588c-9124-0ef5b93698a5', 'rg12-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Eight Sizes for a Nation$q$, $q$Before the 1870s, almost every shirt or coat sold in the fictional nation of Velloria was made for one particular person. A tailor measured each customer and cut the cloth to fit, so clothing was costly and slow to obtain. Ready-made clothing existed, but it fit so badly that most buyers avoided it.

The change began with the army, not the clothing trade. In 1872, the Vellorian army needed uniforms for forty thousand new recruits faster than tailors could measure them. An officer named Haruto Lindqvist ordered every recruit's chest, waist and arm length recorded, and when he studied the records he found that the measurements clustered: most of the men fell into a small number of body shapes. Lindqvist's staff designed uniforms in eight sizes, and the factories that sewed them learned to cut dozens of identical pieces at once.

When the army's orders ended in 1878, those factories had machines, trained workers and a size chart, but no customer. Their owners turned to the public, advertising suits in the army's eight sizes at a third of a tailor's price. Shops that sold only ready-made clothing opened in the larger towns, and for the first time a clerk or farmhand could buy a suit off a rack.

Tailors lost much of their trade, but not all of it. Lindqvist's chart had been built from young men, so it fit older men, women and children poorly. Many tailors survived by doing alterations, adjusting ready-made clothing to fit customers the chart did not describe. A tailor named Esperanza Duvall went further: she spent five years measuring the women who came to her shop and published her own size chart in 1891, which several factories adopted for women's coats.

Size charts in Velloria are still revised every few decades as bodies and fashions change. Each revision traces back, through Duvall's chart and its successors, to a set of army records that were never meant to clothe anyone but soldiers.$q$, 'draft')
