-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e37da047-2bee-568c-af25-28a37df398a5', 'rg12-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Measuring in a Common Language$q$, $q$In the eighteenth century, a merchant traveling across France might encounter hundreds of different units of measurement. A unit of length in one town could be noticeably longer than the unit with the same name a day's ride away. Many of these units were arbitrary, chosen by local custom or by the size of some long-forgotten official's foot rather than by any consistent principle.

The confusion was more than an inconvenience. Buyers suspected sellers of cheating, tax collectors and farmers argued over how much grain a bushel really held, and builders struggled to follow plans drawn in a neighboring region. Reformers complained that the old system was provincial, rooted in the narrow habits of individual districts and unsuited to a nation that traded across its whole territory and beyond.

The need for fair trade provided the impetus for change. In the 1790s, a group of scientists set out to create units based on nature rather than on tradition. They defined the meter as a fraction of the distance from the North Pole to the equator, and they built the other units, including those for volume and mass, from that single foundation. Because the units were related by powers of ten, converting between them required only moving a decimal point.

Designing the system was one challenge; persuading people to use it was another. Officials moved to codify the new units, writing them into formal regulations so that they would carry the force of law. Many shopkeepers and farmers, however, proved intransigent. They refused to give up the measures their grandparents had used, and for decades the old names lingered in markets despite every order to abandon them.

Eventually the advantages became hard to ignore. Over the following century, country after country adopted the system, and today scientists around the world share its units.$q$, 'draft')
