-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d3f73304-a08e-58b6-95d5-044d1fa76b4a', 'rg12-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Round Five$q$, $q$By the fifth round of the regional chess tournament, Priya had learned to read her opponents before she read the board. Some drummed their fingers when worried; others sighed theatrically after every move. Her opponent this afternoon, a tall boy named Teodor, gave away nothing. His face was inscrutable, as blank and unreadable as a closed door, and after twenty minutes she had no idea whether he thought he was winning.

Priya had won her first four games, and she could feel herself growing complacent. She had begun to assume that victory was simply what happened to her now, and that assumption made her careless. Twice she nearly left her bishop unprotected before catching the mistake at the last second.

Then Teodor did something audacious. He pushed a pawn forward into a square where she could capture it for free, an offer so bold that she stared at it for a full minute. Her coach called this kind of move a gambit: a player deliberately gives something up, hoping to gain a stronger position in exchange.

Priya took the pawn. Within six moves she understood the price. Teodor's pieces poured through the gap the pawn had left, and her king, which had seemed safe, was suddenly surrounded by threats.

She considered resigning. The position looked hopeless, and part of her wanted to capitulate now and spare herself the slow embarrassment of being checkmated. But her coach's rule was firm: never surrender a game until you have searched every move. She bent over the board and searched. On the third pass she found it, a quiet knight retreat that blocked two attacks at once.

The game ended in a draw an hour later. Walking out, Teodor finally smiled. "I thought you would give up," he said.$q$, 'draft')
