-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3ff382a5-a9f8-573d-b837-4003a3af3d49', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Letting the Tide Back In$q$, $q$For nearly a century, a row of wooden tide gates at the mouth of Harrowford Creek held back the sea. Farmers built the gates in the 1920s so that salt water could not flood the low meadows where they grazed cattle, and the gates opened only on falling tides to let fresh water drain out. Over the decades, the meadows behind the gates sank. Marsh soil is built partly from plant roots and partly from sediment that the tides carry in, and once the tides were blocked, the roots decayed and no new sediment arrived. By the time the last farm closed, parts of the meadow sat almost a meter lower than the marsh on the seaward side of the gates.

In 2015 the regional conservation board replaced the old gates with a wide culvert that lets tides flow freely in and out. Hydrologist Amara Lindqvist led a team that measured the site every spring for eight years. Within three years, salt-tolerant cordgrass had spread across roughly half of the former pasture. The team's sediment traps showed the meadow surface rising about six millimeters a year, roughly twice the rate recorded on the undisturbed marsh nearby.

Not every result was welcome. Mosquito counts rose sharply in the first two summers, when shallow pools of stagnant water formed in the sunken areas. Counts fell again after the tide began to reach those pools twice a day and small fish moved in. Lindqvist also notes that a few rare freshwater plants that had survived in the drained meadow have now disappeared.

The board now faces a choice about a second set of gates on Penhallow Creek, a few kilometers north. That meadow has sunk even further, and some engineers worry that opening it all at once would let the sea scour channels deep enough to undermine a nearby road. Lindqvist has recommended opening the Penhallow gates in stages over several years, while inspecting the road embankment after every major storm.$q$, 'draft')
