-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e86ed4d8-7a42-5be6-9960-72dce29e083d', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Opinion: The Fines Were Never Worth It$q$, $q$Three years ago, the Brindlecombe Public Library stopped charging fines on overdue books. As a member of the library board, I voted for that change, and some of my neighbors still tell me it was a mistake. They worry that without a penalty, borrowers will keep books for months and the shelves will empty. The library's own records suggest that worry was mostly misplaced.

In the year before fines ended, the library collected just over 9,000 dollars in overdue charges. That sounds like useful money until you set it beside the cost of collecting it. Staff spent hours each week handling small payments, answering disputes and mailing reminder letters, and the board's finance committee estimated that this work cost more than the fines brought in. Meanwhile, about 1,400 cardholders had their borrowing blocked because they owed more than ten dollars. Many of those accounts belonged to children, whose families had simply stopped coming.

Since the change, more than 600 of those blocked cardholders have returned and borrowed again. The share of books returned within two weeks of their due date has stayed almost the same, around 94 percent. Books still go missing, and borrowers who never return an item are still billed for its replacement, so the library has not given up every tool for getting its property back.

I will admit one disappointment. A small group of popular new novels now comes back later than before, and the waiting lists for those titles have grown. The board is testing shorter loan periods for high-demand books, which seems to me a fairer fix than bringing back fines for everyone.

A library exists to put books in readers' hands. A policy that kept more than a thousand people away in exchange for a few thousand dollars, most of it spent on collecting itself, was working against that purpose.$q$, 'draft')
