-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('62364ea3-50e5-5cde-823f-94a681738d37', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the Date on the Package$q$, $q$Many packaged foods carry a date, but the words in front of that date matter more than most shoppers realize. A "use by" date on fresh meat or fish is about safety, and food should not be eaten after it passes. A "best before" date on crackers, cereal or canned beans is about quality: after that date the food may be a little stale or less flavorful, but it is usually still safe if it has been stored properly.

Researchers at the Tarnwell Institute asked 420 households in the city of Pellham Reach to keep a diary of every food item they threw away over four weeks. The households also answered questions about how they understood date labels. Nearly half said they treated "best before" and "use by" as meaning the same thing, and those households discarded about a third more food by weight than households that knew the difference. The items most often thrown away unopened were yogurt, bread and packaged salad.

The team then tried a simple test. Half of the households received a one-page guide explaining the two labels, along with a fridge magnet repeating the main points. Over the next four weeks, those households cut their unopened waste by about a fifth, while the other households showed almost no change. Lead researcher Hana Vukovic cautions that families who know they are being studied may behave more carefully than usual, so the real effect of a guide could be smaller. She also notes that the diaries depended on honest reporting, and some households admitted to forgetting entries on busy days.

Some food makers in the region have begun replacing "best before" with the phrase "best if used by," hoping it will sound less like a warning. The researchers have not yet tested whether shoppers read the new phrase any differently.$q$, 'draft')
